{
  "skillId": "time-complexity",
  "title": "Big-O: how code scales",
  "minutes": 5,
  "version": 1,
  "steps": [
    {
      "id": "hook",
      "type": "hook",
      "title": "Fast for 10, frozen for 10 lakh",
      "body": "Your code passes the sample tests, then **times out** on the hidden ones. That is what interviewers test with *\"what is the complexity?\"*: not how fast code is today, but how the work **grows** as the input grows.\n\nIn five minutes you’ll count steps as they happen and see why a nested loop explodes."
    },
    {
      "id": "p1",
      "type": "predict",
      "prompt": "What does this program print?",
      "code": "public class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        int n = 4;\n        int steps = 0;\n        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) { //~ The outer loop runs n times.\n            for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) { //~ For EACH outer round, the inner loop runs n times too.\n                steps++; //~ Count one unit of work.\n            }\n        }\n        System.out.println(steps); //~ n × n = 16. Double n and the steps quadruple. That is O(n²).\n    }\n}",
      "options": [
        {
          "text": "8",
          "why": "The loops multiply, they don’t add: the whole inner loop runs again every outer round."
        },
        {
          "text": "20",
          "why": "Count the steps++ lines that run: 4 rounds × 4 = 16."
        },
        {
          "text": "16",
          "why": "Right: the inner loop runs 4 times for each of the 4 outer rounds, so 4 × 4."
        },
        {
          "text": "4",
          "why": "steps++ is inside BOTH loops."
        }
      ],
      "answer": 2
    },
